Toward a Thermodynamic Monetary Rule: Exergy, Computation, and Physical Productive Capacity as Constraints on Monetary Policy

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

[v3.1] We develop a formal framework for a time-varying Friedman $k$-percent rule indexed to a physically-constrained measure of productive capacity. The rule is not a convertibility standard. The framework rests on six structural elements. First, thermodynamic and material constraints define a compact feasible production set, and the capacity object is its radial function in a pre-registered reference direction — a Liebig-style minimum over binding constraints. Second, this makes the domain of validity of the energy rule a theorem rather than a stipulation: an energy-only representation of capacity is sufficient when energy binds, and structurally impossible when the other constraints vary independently. Third, a Kalman filter on a local-linear trend plus AR(1) cycle generates a genuine trade-off in the anchoring horizon $\tau$; the loss is strictly convex in $\tau$ and the unique interior optimum admits a closed form in terms of the Lambert $W$ function. Fourth, under cash-in-advance, a variance-and-drift decomposition gives the exact dominance condition of the capacity rule over benchmarks, and shows where compute and capital enter. Fifth, the resource, capital, and compute stocks are given explicit intertemporal dynamics, closing the monetary transmission chain. Sixth, falsification uses paired differences per calibration draw with a pre-registered win-share per channel, C1 (interest rate) being the primary test. The contribution is scientific even if the main hypothesis fails. [v3.1]

---

1. Introduction

1.1 The problem

The proposal to anchor money to energy has recurred since Soddy (1926) and has re-emerged in academic (Sgouridis & Csala 2014; Krätzig-Ahlert & Fuders 2026; Schlaudt 2025) and commercial forms. The literature has not produced a theory satisfying simultaneously:

1. Thermodynamic soundness — respecting Georgescu-Roegen's (1971) demonstration that energy is a necessary but not sufficient condition of value;
2. Operational measurability — with characterized error that propagates through binding constraints only;
3. Falsifiable dynamics — yielding pre-registered predictions about stability under shocks, benchmarked against established monetary rules;
4. A well-defined trade-off in the anchoring horizon $\tau$, giving a genuine interior optimum;
5. A closed monetary transmission chain: physical stocks → capacity → monetary target → instrument → real allocations.

1.2 Contribution

We contribute:

· A five-level architecture: physical state → feasible set → radial capacity → measured capacity → monetary rule (§3–§7);
· Compactness of the feasible set and existence of the radial capacity function (§4);
· The bottleneck theorem (§4.4);
· A structural non-reducibility proposition stated as a regime-dependent result, not an iff, and requiring independent variation of non-energy constraints (§4.5);
· A Kalman-filter forecast operator with a genuine $\tau$-trade-off, closed-form $\tau^*$ in terms of $W(\cdot)$ (§6);
· A variance-and-drift decomposition under cash-in-advance, giving the dominance condition, with explicit dynamics for $R, K, C$ so the condition closes (§8);
· A paired-difference falsification protocol indexed by channel, C1 as primary (§10).

1.3 Terminology

[v3.1] We do not call our rule a "standard." It is a time-varying Friedman $k$-percent rule indexed to a physical proxy of potential output. It is opposite to Selgin's productivity norm: Selgin advocates constant money with falling prices as productivity rises; our rule tracks capacity growth with a stable price target.

[v3.1] We do not call capacity "sustainable." v0.1 uses physical capacity. Sustainability requires an intertemporal resource dynamic that is only introduced in §5.4 as a partially-specified extension. Calling $P^{\max}$ "sustainable" before specifying the intertemporal problem would smuggle a normative constraint into the definition.

[v3.1] We do not call $P^{\max}$ the "Minkowski gauge." It is the radial function of the feasible set in a pre-registered direction (§4.3, Remark 2).

1.4 Structure

§2 positions the work. §3 states axioms. §4 defines the feasible set, proves compactness, defines the radial capacity function, proves the bottleneck theorem, states the structural non-reducibility proposition. §5 closes the resource, capital, and compute dynamics. §6 develops the forecast operator. §7 specifies monetary rules and implementation. §8 proves the decomposition. §9 states the loss. §10 specifies falsification. §11 states hypotheses. §12 limitations. §13 concludes.

