2. Literature and positioning

2.1 Thermodynamic foundations

Georgescu-Roegen (1971). Low entropy is a necessary but not sufficient condition for value. Any theory reducing value to energy is refuted.

[v3.1] Ayres & Warr (2005, 2009); Warr, Ayres & Eisenmenger (2010). Warr et al. (2010) document historical correlations between exergy / useful work and economic development across four countries, including declining exergy intensity of GDP. These papers do not establish that exergy is the binding constraint of production. They document a robust empirical regularity. Our framework offers a regime-based interpretation of why exergy-output correlations may be strong in some states and weaker in others (§4.5). This is an interpretation, not a conclusion drawn from Ayres–Warr.

2.2 Monetary-energy literature

Soddy (1926). Conceptual ancestor.
Sgouridis & Kennedy (2010). Energy Policy. Proposal for a parallel energy currency in Masdar City. Conceptual case study.
Sgouridis & Csala (2014). Frontiers in Energy Research. Energy-referenced currency; theoretical.
Schlaudt (2025). Studies in History and Philosophy of Science. The relation between physical and monetary metrics. Not a monetary architecture.
Krätzig-Ahlert & Fuders (2026). American Journal of Economics and Sociology. Exergy as numéraire; monetary creation bounded by annual exergy harvest; natural zero interest rate. Our contribution is distinguished by its capacity-constraint focus: Krätzig-Ahlert & Fuders index the stock of money to a flow of exergy; we index the growth of money to the growth of a capacity object derivable from the geometry of the feasible set.

2.3 Benchmark monetary rules

Friedman (1960). $k$-percent rule: $\Delta\log M_t = k$ constant. Our rule is the time-varying generalization.
McCallum (1988, 1999). NGDP-targeting with velocity and price-gap corrections. Our R0' benchmark.
Selgin (1997). Productivity norm: constant money, falling prices as productivity rises. Structural opposite.

2.4 Real-time measurement

Orphanides (2001); Orphanides & van Norden (2002). Real-time output-gap estimates are cycle-correlated biased. Our anchor inherits this bias.

