3. Axiomatic foundations

3.1 Primitives

Discrete time $t \in \{0,1,\dots,T\}$. Physical sub-state:
S_t^{\text{phys}} = (E_t, X_t, C_t, K_t, R_t, \eta_t) \in \mathcal{S}^{\text{phys}} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^6_+

$E_t \geq 0$: available primary exergy flow.
$X_t \geq 0$: installed energy infrastructure (capacity).
$C_t \geq 0$: aggregate installed computational capacity (normalized).
$K_t \geq 0$: physical capital.
$R_t \geq 0$: critical resource stock.
$\eta_t \in (0,1]$: conversion efficiency.

Production vector: $Y_t = (Y_{g,t}, Y_{s,t}, Y_{e,t}) \in \mathbb{R}^3_+$.

3.2 Axioms

A1 (Thermodynamic bound).
\varepsilon \cdot Y_t \leq \eta_t E_t


$\varepsilon = (\varepsilon_g, \varepsilon_s, \varepsilon_e) \in \mathbb{R}^3_{++}$: primary-exergy intensities on a total (direct + upstream) basis.

A2 (Material bound). For goods:
Y_{g,t} \leq g(R_t, \dot{R}_t)


$g$ increasing in $R_t$, non-increasing in $|\dot{R}_t|$ when $\dot{R}_t < 0$.

A3 (Compute bound, smooth). For services:
Y_{s,t} \leq A_{s,t} K_{s,t}^{\alpha_s} L_{s,t}^{\beta_s} s(C_t)


$s: \mathbb{R}_+ \to \mathbb{R}_+$ continuous, strictly increasing, concave, $s(0) = 0$.

A4 (Bounded measurement with bias). $P_t^{\text{eff}}$ unobservable. Only $\hat{S}_t^{\text{phys}} = S_t^{\text{phys}} + \xi_t$ observable, with $\xi_t$ possibly biased and possibly correlated with the cycle.

A5 (Institutional selection, exogenous). $P_t^{\text{eff}} = \Phi_{\text{inst}}(P_t^{\max}; \theta_G)$, one-parameter family $\Phi_{\text{inst}}(P^{\max}; \theta_G) = \theta_G P^{\max}$.

---

4. Feasible set and radial capacity

4.1 Feasible set

Definition 1.
\mathcal{F}_t = \left\{ Y \in \mathbb{R}^3_+ : \text{A1, A2, A3 hold} \right\}


Comprehensive from below.

4.2 Compactness

Theorem 1 (Compactness). Under A1–A3 with continuous right-hand sides, $\mathcal{F}_t$ is compact.

Proof. Boundedness: A1 gives $\varepsilon \cdot Y \leq \eta_t \bar{E} < \infty$; $\varepsilon \in \mathbb{R}^3_{++}$ implies $Y_j \leq \eta_t \bar{E}/\varepsilon_j$. Closedness: A1–A3 are weak inequalities with continuous LHS; their intersection is closed. $\square$

4.3 Radial capacity function

[v3.1] Definition 2 (Radial capacity function). Fix $y^* = (y_g^*, y_s^*, y_e^*) \in \mathbb{R}^3_{++}$. Define:
\boxed{ \rho_{\mathcal{F}_t}(y^*) = \sup \left\{ \lambda \geq 0 : \lambda y^* \in \mathcal{F}_t \right\} }


We write $P_t^{\text{phys}} := \rho_{\mathcal{F}_t}(y^*)$.

[v3.1] Remark 2 (Notation and relation to the Minkowski gauge). The object $\rho_{\mathcal{F}_t}(y^*)$ is the radial function of $\mathcal{F}_t$ in the direction $y^*$. The Minkowski functional (gauge) of $\mathcal{F}_t$ is
p_{\mathcal{F}_t}(y) = \inf\{\lambda > 0 : y \in \lambda \mathcal{F}_t\}


and, for $y^* \neq 0$, one has $p_{\mathcal{F}_t}(y^*) = 1/\rho_{\mathcal{F}_t}(y^*)$ provided $\rho_{\mathcal{F}_t}(y^*) \in (0, \infty)$. We use the radial function because it is the object that directly scales the reference bundle. The link to the Minkowski gauge is stated as a mathematical fact, not as an identification.

Proposition 1 (Existence and properties).

· $\Lambda_t = \{\lambda \geq 0 : \lambda y^* \in \mathcal{F}_t\}$ is non-empty ($0 \in \mathcal{F}_t$).
· $\Lambda_t$ closed (preimage of closed $\mathcal{F}_t$ under continuous $\lambda \mapsto \lambda y^*$).
· $\Lambda_t$ bounded (Theorem 1).
· Hence $\sup$ is attained.
· Comprehensiveness gives $\Lambda_t = [0, P_t^{\text{phys}}]$.

Proof. Direct. $\square$

4.4 The bottleneck theorem

Definition 3. Let $\varepsilon \cdot y^* = \varepsilon_g y_g^* + \varepsilon_s y_s^* + \varepsilon_e y_e^*$.

Theorem 2 (Bottleneck). Under A1–A3:
P_t^{\text{phys}} = \min \left\{ \frac{\eta_t E_t}{\varepsilon \cdot y^*}, \; \frac{g(R_t, \dot{R}_t)}{y_g^*}, \; \frac{A_{s,t} K_{s,t}^{\alpha_s} L_{s,t}^{\beta_s} s(C_t)}{y_s^*} \right\}

Proof. $\lambda y^*$ satisfies A1 iff $\lambda(\varepsilon \cdot y^*) \leq \eta_t E_t$. Similarly for A2, A3. All three constraints bind; $\lambda$ is the min of the three upper bounds. $\square$

4.5 Structural non-reducibility

[v3.1] Proposition 2 (Sufficiency of energy-only representation when energy binds). If the energy constraint is strictly the minimum of Theorem 2 on a set $\Sigma \subseteq \mathcal{S}^{\text{phys}}$, then on $\Sigma$:
P_t^{\text{phys}} = \frac{\eta_t E_t}{\varepsilon \cdot y^*}


which is a function of $(E_t, \eta_t)$ alone. An energy-only rule of the form $\Delta \log M_t = \Delta \log(\eta_t E_t)$ reproduces the growth of $P_t^{\text{phys}}$ on $\Sigma$.

Proof. Direct substitution. $\square$

[v3.1] Proposition 3 (Structural non-reducibility under independent variation). Let $\Sigma \subseteq \mathcal{S}^{\text{phys}}$ be such that:
(i) on $\Sigma$, the material or compute constraint is the binding one;
(ii) the conditional supports of $R_t$, $K_t$, $C_t$ given $E_t$ are non-degenerate on $\Sigma$;
(iii) the functions $g, s$ are not constant in their arguments on $\Sigma$.

Then there exists no function $h: \mathbb{R}_+ \to \mathbb{R}_+$ such that
h(E_t) = P_t^{\text{phys}} \quad \text{for all } S_t^{\text{phys}} \in \Sigma.

Proof. By (i), on $\Sigma$ the minimum in Theorem 2 is achieved by $g/y_g^*$ or $A K^\alpha L^\beta s(C)/y_s^*$. By (ii) and (iii), this quantity varies while $E_t$ is held fixed, so no function of $E_t$ alone can equal it throughout $\Sigma$. $\square$

[v3.1] Remark 3 (Correction of v3). The prior draft asserted an "iff". The correct statement is asymmetric: sufficiency is unconditional, non-reducibility requires independent variation. Without (ii), a degenerate co-movement between $R$ and $E$ could allow an energy-only representation even when energy does not bind. The proposition is now stated as a structural result under an explicit variation assumption, not as an unconditional iff.

[v3.1] Remark 4 (Interpretation of Ayres–Warr). Prop. 2–3 together give a regime interpretation of why exergy-output correlations can be strong in some periods and weaker in others: energy binds in some states, and the correlations are strong there. This is an interpretation of our framework, not a claim about the Ayres–Warr data.

