5. Dynamics of $R, K, C$ [v3.1]

Prior drafts treated $R_t, K_t, C_t$ as exogenous processes, which made the monetary transmission chain $i_t \to I_t \to K_{t+1}, C_{t+1}$ incomplete. We close it.

5.1 Resource dynamics

Non-renewable benchmark:
R_{t+1} = R_t - Q_t, \qquad Q_t = \text{extraction at } t

With regeneration:
R_{t+1} = R_t - Q_t + \mathcal{G}(R_t)


$\mathcal{G}$ concave, $\mathcal{G}(0) = 0$.

The extraction flow $Q_t$ is bounded by A2: $g(R_t, \dot{R}_t)$ is non-increasing in $|\dot{R}_t|$ when $\dot{R}_t < 0$.

5.2 Capital dynamics

K_{t+1} = (1 - \delta_K) K_t + I_t


$I_t \geq 0$ investment, $\delta_K \in (0,1)$ depreciation.

5.3 Compute dynamics

C_{t+1} = (1 - \delta_C) C_t + I_t^C


$I_t^C \geq 0$ compute investment, $\delta_C \in (0,1)$ depreciation.

5.4 Sustainability (deferred)

[v3.1] The system above gives physical feasibility. A sustainable capacity would require:
R_{t+1} \geq 0 \quad \forall t, \qquad \text{or} \qquad R_t \geq \underline{R} \text{ for a pre-registered floor } \underline{R}


plus an intertemporal welfare criterion over resource use. v0.1 does not impose this. The title and abstract use "physical," not "sustainable." The sustainable version is deferred to an extension (§12).

5.5 Monetary transmission closure [v3.1]

Chain. Under C1, the rule targets $M_t^{\text{target}}$ but the central bank operates via the interest rate. We need an implementation equation:
i_t = \mathcal{I}\left( M_t^{\text{target}}, D_t, \pi_t \right)


where $D_t$ is real money demand. For v0.1:
i_t = i_t^n - \kappa \left( \Delta \log M_t - \Delta \log M_t^{\text{target}} \right), \qquad \kappa > 0


with $i_t^n$ the natural rate. The central bank raises the rate when money growth exceeds target.

Then $i_t \to I_t \to K_{t+1}, C_{t+1} \to S_{t+1}^{\text{phys}} \to P_{t+1}^{\text{phys}}$, closing the chain.

