6. Forecast operator and the $\tau$-trade-off

6.1 State-space

$p_t = \log P_t^{\text{mes}}$. Model:
p_t = \mu_t + c_t, \quad \mu_{t+1} = \mu_t + g_t + \eta_t^\mu, \quad g_{t+1} = g_t + \zeta_t, \quad c_{t+1} = \phi c_t + \nu_t


$0 < \phi < 1$. Innovations $(\eta^\mu, \zeta, \nu)$ mutually uncorrelated with variances $(\sigma_\mu^2, \sigma_g^2, \sigma_c^2)$.

6.2 Forecast

\log \hat{P}_t(\tau) = \hat{\mu}_t + \tau \hat{g}_t + \phi^\tau \hat{c}_t

6.3 Rule

R3 (real-time capacity rule). [v3.1]
\Delta \log M_t = \log \hat{P}_t(\tau) - \log \hat{P}_{t-1}(\tau)

R2 (oracle capacity rule). [v3.1] Same expression with $\hat{P}$ replaced by $P^{\text{true}}$ (unobservable, used as benchmark):
\Delta \log M_t = \log P_t^{\text{true}}(\tau) - \log P_{t-1}^{\text{true}}(\tau)


The gap $R2 - R3$ measures the cost of informational imperfection. Prior drafts mislabelled R2 as "perfect forecast" and R3 as "filter uncertainty." This was inaccurate: the filter always produces uncertainty.

6.4 Variance of capacity-rule money growth

\operatorname{Var}(\Delta \log M_t^C) \approx V_0 + A e^{-2a\tau} + B \tau^2


with $a = \ln(1/\phi) > 0$. Numerically the cross-covariances in $\Sigma$ add linear terms $C \tau$; these are retained in the numerical computation.

6.5 Strict convexity and $\tau^*$ [v3.1]

Theorem 4 (Existence and uniqueness of $\tau^*$, stylized). Assume $A > 0$, $B > 0$, and $|C|$ small relative to $A, B$ (cross-covariance dominance rule). Then $V(\tau) = V_0 + A e^{-2a\tau} + B \tau^2$ is strictly convex and has a unique minimizer
\boxed{ \tau^* = \frac{1}{2a} \, W\!\left( \frac{2 A a}{B} \right) }


where $W(\cdot)$ is the principal branch of the Lambert $W$ function.

Proof. $V'(\tau) = -2Aa e^{-2a\tau} + 2B\tau$. Setting to zero:
B \tau = A a e^{-2a\tau} \implies \tau e^{2a\tau} = \frac{A a}{B} \implies 2a \tau e^{2a\tau} = \frac{2Aa}{B} \implies 2a\tau = W\!\left(\frac{2Aa}{B}\right)


$V''(\tau) = 4 A a^2 e^{-2a\tau} + 2B > 0$ for all $\tau \geq 0$, so strict convexity and uniqueness. $\square$

[v3.1] Remark 5 (Correction of v3). Prior draft stated $\tau^* = \frac{1}{2\ln(1/\phi)} \log(A \ln(1/\phi)/B)$. This is incorrect: the correct solution of $V'(\tau) = 0$ involves the Lambert $W$ function, not the logarithm. The qualitative conclusions (strict convexity, existence, uniqueness) are unchanged.

[v3.1] Remark 6 (Numerical treatment). When $C \tau$ is included, $\tau^*$ is computed numerically. Strict convexity is checked on the domain $[\tau_{\min}, \tau_{\max}]$ for each calibration draw.

