7. Monetary rules and implementation

7.1 Taxonomy

R0 (Discretionary fiat). Taylor rule:
i_t = r^n + \pi^* + \phi_\pi(\pi_t - \pi^*) + \phi_y \tilde{Y}_t, \quad \phi_\pi > 1

R0' (NGDP-targeting fiat). McCallum-type:
\Delta \log M_t = \Delta \log \bar{Y}_t - \Delta \log V_t + \lambda(\log P^* - \log P_t)

R0'' (Friedman $k$-percent). $\Delta \log M_t = k$ constant.

[v3.1] R1 (Energy rule). The benchmark corresponding to our energy constraint is not $E_t$ but useful energy:
\Delta \log M_t = \Delta \log E_t^{\text{useful}}, \qquad E_t^{\text{useful}} = \eta_t E_t


Prior drafts used $E_t$, ignoring $\eta_t$, which is asymmetric relative to Theorem 2.

R2 (Oracle capacity rule). §6.3.
R3 (Real-time capacity rule). §6.3.

7.2 Transmission channels

C1 (Interest rate). $\Delta \log M_t^{\text{target}} \to i_t$ via the implementation equation (§5.5), then $i_t \to I_t \to K_{t+1}, C_{t+1} \to Y_{t+1}$.

C2 (Cash-in-advance). $P_t Y_t = M_t$.

C3 (Credit). $B_t = \Psi(M_t, K_t, \rho_t)$.

[v3.1] Remark 7 (Correction of v3). The prior draft left C1 open: R2/R3 defined $M_t$ directly while C1 required the central bank to choose $i_t$. §5.5 closes this with an explicit implementation equation. Without it, C1 is under-identified.

