10. Falsification protocol

10.1 Calibration sampling

[v3.1] 5-dimensional Sobol design over $(\eta, \delta, c_{\min}, \kappa_\varepsilon, \omega)$:

· $\eta \in (0,1]$,
· $\delta$: exponent in $s(C_t) = (C_t / c_{\min})^\delta$,
· $c_{\min} > 0$,
· $\kappa_\varepsilon \in [0.8, 1.2]$,
· $\omega \in [-0.2, 0.2]$.

256 Sobol draws. 192 train ($\Theta_{\text{train}}$), 64 test ($\Theta_{\text{test}}$). Split pre-registered.

[v3.1] Remark 10 (Correction of v3). Prior drafts referenced "five pre-registered calibrations". This was superseded by the 256-draw design. The obsolete reference is removed.

10.2 Falsification conditions [v3.1]

F1 (Primary failure, per channel). For each channel $c \in \{C1, C2, C3\}$ and each draw $\theta \in \Theta_{\text{test}}$:
\Delta_{P,c}(\theta) = L_{P,c}(\tau^*(\theta); \theta) - \min\left( L_{F,c}(\theta), L_{E,c}(\theta), L_{R0'',c}(\theta) \right)


q_{P,c} = \frac{\#\{\theta \in \Theta_{\text{test}} : \Delta_{P,c}(\theta) < 0\}}{|\Theta_{\text{test}}|}

Primary criterion. $q_{P,C1} \geq 0.75$. Rationale: C1 is the standard New-Keynesian channel; C2 is explicitly favorable to any growth rule; choosing C1 as primary prevents C2 from rescuing a rule that fails in the standard channel.

Robustness criteria. $q_{P,C2}, q_{P,C3}$ reported as robustness.

Bootstrap. 95% CI on each $q_{P,c}$ from 10,000 resamples.

F2 (Fragility). Rank-order changes under $\pm 20\%$ perturbation of physical parameters.

F3 (Epistemic fragility). Sensitivity of $L_P - L_F$ to filter variances in the small-error regime.

F4 (Non-result). $\mathbf{L}_P$ and $\mathbf{L}_F$ Pareto-incomparable in majority of test draws.

10.3 Shock scenarios

# Shock Magnitude Purpose
S1 Energy supply −20 % Energy robustness
S2 Compute efficiency +100 % Technological shock
S3 Capital stock −30 % Productive destruction
S4 Critical resource −30 % Material constraint
S5 Aggregate demand +20 % Procyclicality test
S6 Filter misspecification +50 % $\sigma_\mu, \sigma_g, \sigma_c$ Epistemic robustness

10.4 Historical backtest

Series: IEA World Energy Balances, Penn World Table, Masanet et al. (2020) compute proxy, FRED output, Philadelphia Fed RTDSM vintages.
Frequency: annual 1970–2023; compute proxy 2010–2018.
Protocol: partial-equilibrium counterfactual money growth under R0', R0'', R1, R2, R3; descriptive complement to simulations.

[v3.1] Remark 11 (Coverage limits). RTDSM does not cover energy, capital, or compute. Masanet et al. cover only 2010–2018. H4 (compute) is tested only in simulation, not in the historical sample.

