12. Extensions and limitations

12.1 Extensions

· Endogenous governance.
· Open economy.
· Transition path.
· Distributional analysis.
· Heterogeneous compute taxonomy.
· Sustainable capacity with $R_{t+1} \geq 0$ constraint and intertemporal welfare criterion.
· Endogenous $K, C$ response to the rule (partially closed in §5).

12.2 Limitations of v0.1

· $\theta_G$ exogenous.
· Compute aggregated as scalar.
· Reference bundle $y^*$ is a convention. [v3.1] The framework has "no free aggregation weights conditional on a pre-registered reference composition." The composition itself uses real value added for goods and services, which is constructed from prices and deflators; it is therefore not weight-free in a deeper sense. Sensitivity via $\omega$ and F2.
· Services energy intensity poorly measured.
· $\varepsilon$ fixes technology.
· Identification of slack constraints requires engineering data.
· A3 smooth form parameterized by $\delta$.
· Historical backtest partial-equilibrium.
· RTDSM does not cover energy, capital, compute; H4 in simulation only.
· Dynamics of $R, K, C$ closed but investment functions $I_t, I_t^C$ specified only at the "chain" level (§5.2–5.3); the microfoundation of investment is deferred.

---

13. Conclusion

We have specified a falsifiable framework for a time-varying Friedman $k$-percent rule indexed to a radial measure of physical productive capacity. The framework:

· Defines capacity as the radial function of the physical feasible set in a pre-registered direction — notation corrected from the erroneous "Minkowski gauge" (Definition 2, Remark 2);
· Proves compactness (Theorem 1);
· Proves the bottleneck theorem (Theorem 2);
· States structural non-reducibility with the necessary independent-variation hypothesis, not as an unconditional iff (Propositions 2–3);
· Derives the Kalman-filter forecast operator with a genuine $\tau$-trade-off, closed-form $\tau^*$ via the Lambert $W$ function, strict convexity, and unique interior optimum (Theorem 4, Remark 5);
· Closes the dynamics of $R, K, C$ and the implementation equation for C1, so that the monetary transmission chain is complete (§5);
· Distinguishes R2 (oracle) from R3 (real-time) as a measure of informational cost;
· Uses useful energy in R1, matching Theorem 2;
· Proves the variance-and-drift decomposition without wrongly cancelling $\operatorname{Var}(\Delta y)$ (Theorem 5, Remark 8);
· Uses channel-indexed falsification with C1 as primary (F1);
· States H4 as a testable hypothesis on $\partial q_P / \partial |\rho_{C,Y}|$.

We make no uniqueness claim beyond Theorem 4's stylized statement. We claim only that the framework is well-specified, falsifiable, and that its main hypothesis can be rejected by pre-registered tests.

