Appendix B — Proof of Theorem 1 (Compactness of the feasible set)

Theorem B.1 (restatement of Theorem 1). Fix $S \in \mathcal{S}^{\text{phys}}$. Define
\mathcal{F} = \left\{ Y \in \mathbb{R}^3_+ : \exists\ (K_g, L_g, E_g, K_s, L_s, E_s) \geq 0 \text{ s.t. A1–A3 hold} \right\}.


Under A.4, $\mathcal{F}$ is compact in $\mathbb{R}^3_+$.

Proof.

Boundedness. A1 gives
\varepsilon \cdot Y \leq \eta E.


Since $\varepsilon \in \mathbb{R}^3_{++}$, this implies $Y_j \leq \eta E / \varepsilon_j$ for each $j \in \{g, s, e\}$. Since $E$ is finite by physical assumption on $\mathcal{S}^{\text{phys}}$ and $\eta \leq 1$, $Y$ is bounded componentwise. Hence $\mathcal{F} \subseteq \prod_j [0, \eta E / \varepsilon_j]$.

Closedness. A1 defines a closed half-space. A2 and A3 are of the form $Y_g \leq g(R, \dot R)$ and $Y_s \leq A_s K_s^{\alpha_s} L_s^{\beta_s} s(C)$, which are upper contour sets of continuous functions on the technology variables, and since the technology variables $(K_s, L_s)$ range over a closed rectangle $[0, \bar K] \times [0, 1]$, these constraints define closed sets in $Y$-space after projecting over the technology variables. Specifically, the set
\{Y : Y_g \leq \sup_{K_g \in [0, \bar K]} g(R, \dot R)\} = \{Y : Y_g \leq g(R, \dot R)\}


is closed (since $g$ is independent of $K_g$ in A2), and
\{Y : Y_s \leq \sup_{K_s \in [0, \bar K], L_s \in [0,1]} A_s K_s^{\alpha_s} L_s^{\beta_s} s(C)\}


is closed by the Weierstrass theorem applied to a continuous function on a compact set; the supremum is attained and finite.

The intersection of A1, A2, A3 in $Y$-space is the intersection of three closed sets, hence closed.

Conclusion. $\mathcal{F}$ is closed and bounded in $\mathbb{R}^3_+$, hence compact. $\square$

Remark B.1. If A2 and A3 are replaced by smooth forms with $K_g$ entering explicitly, the same argument applies provided the technology variables range over a compact set. If unbounded, we must either assume a technological upper bound $\bar K < \infty$ or a resource constraint that bounds $K$ endogenously. We adopt the former convention throughout.

