Appendix C — Proof of Proposition 1 (Existence and properties of the radial function)

Proposition C.1 (restatement of Proposition 1). Under A.4 and the comprehensiveness of $\mathcal{F}$ from below, the set $\Lambda = \{\lambda \geq 0 : \lambda y^* \in \mathcal{F}\}$ is a compact interval $[0, \rho_{\mathcal{F}}(y^*)]$ with $\rho_{\mathcal{F}}(y^*) \in (0, \infty)$.

Proof.

Non-emptiness. Since $0 \in \mathcal{F}$ (by comprehensiveness from below and $0 \geq 0$ trivially satisfying A1–A3 with zero production), $0 \in \Lambda$.

Closedness. The map $\lambda \mapsto \lambda y^*$ is continuous from $\mathbb{R}_+$ to $\mathbb{R}^3_+$. Since $\mathcal{F}$ is closed (Theorem B.1) and $\Lambda = (\lambda \mapsto \lambda y^*)^{-1}(\mathcal{F}) \cap \mathbb{R}_+$, $\Lambda$ is closed.

Boundedness. Suppose for contradiction $\Lambda$ unbounded. Then there exists a sequence $\lambda_n \to \infty$ with $\lambda_n y^* \in \mathcal{F}$. But $\|\lambda_n y^*\| = \lambda_n \|y^*\| \to \infty$, contradicting the boundedness of $\mathcal{F}$ (Theorem B.1). Hence $\Lambda$ is bounded.

Interval property. Comprehensiveness from below means $Y \in \mathcal{F}$ and $0 \leq Y' \leq Y$ implies $Y' \in \mathcal{F}$. Given $\lambda \in \Lambda$ and $0 \leq \lambda' \leq \lambda$, we have $\lambda' y^* \leq \lambda y^*$ componentwise and $\lambda' y^* \geq 0$, so $\lambda' y^* \in \mathcal{F}$. Hence $\Lambda = [0, \rho]$ where $\rho = \sup \Lambda = \max \Lambda$ (attained since $\Lambda$ is closed and bounded).

Positivity. Since $y^* \in \mathbb{R}^3_{++}$ and A1 permits $\lambda y^* \in \mathcal{F}$ for small $\lambda > 0$, $\rho > 0$. Finite by boundedness. $\square$

Remark C.1. The quantity $\rho_{\mathcal{F}}(y^*)$ is the radial function of $\mathcal{F}$ in direction $y^*$. Its relation to the Minkowski gauge is given in Appendix J.

