Appendix D — Proof of Theorem 2 (Bottleneck)

Theorem D.1 (restatement of Theorem 2). Under A1–A3 and A.4,
\rho_{\mathcal{F}}(y^*) = \min\left\{ \frac{\eta E}{\varepsilon \cdot y^*}, \; \frac{g(R, \dot R)}{y_g^*}, \; \frac{A_s \bar K^{\alpha_s} s(C)}{y_s^*} \right\}


where $\bar K$ is the technological bound on $K_s$ (Appendix B, Remark B.1).

Proof.

We characterize $\Lambda = \{\lambda \geq 0 : \lambda y^* \in \mathcal{F}\}$ as an intersection of half-lines.

From A1. $\lambda y^*$ satisfies A1 iff
\varepsilon \cdot (\lambda y^*) = \lambda (\varepsilon \cdot y^*) \leq \eta E,


i.e., $\lambda \leq \eta E / (\varepsilon \cdot y^*)$. Set $\lambda_1 = \eta E / (\varepsilon \cdot y^*)$.

From A2. $\lambda y^*$ satisfies A2 (only constraining the goods component) iff
\lambda y_g^* \leq g(R, \dot R),


i.e., $\lambda \leq g(R, \dot R) / y_g^*$. Set $\lambda_2 = g(R, \dot R) / y_g^*$.

From A3. $\lambda y^*$ satisfies A3 (only constraining the services component) iff
\lambda y_s^* \leq \max_{K_s \in [0, \bar K], L_s \in [0,1]} A_s K_s^{\alpha_s} L_s^{\beta_s} s(C).


The max over the compact set $[0, \bar K] \times [0, 1]$ is attained at $K_s = \bar K$, $L_s = 1$ (monotonicity of the Cobb-Douglas kernel in each argument). Thus
\lambda \leq A_s \bar K^{\alpha_s} s(C) / y_s^*.


Set $\lambda_3 = A_s \bar K^{\alpha_s} s(C) / y_s^*$.

Combining. A vector $\lambda y^*$ satisfies all three constraints iff $\lambda \leq \min(\lambda_1, \lambda_2, \lambda_3)$. Comprehensiveness from below ensures $\Lambda = [0, \min(\lambda_1, \lambda_2, \lambda_3)]$, so $\rho_{\mathcal{F}}(y^*) = \min(\lambda_1, \lambda_2, \lambda_3)$. $\square$

Corollary D.1 (Binding constraint). $\rho_{\mathcal{F}}(y^*)$ is attained by the constraint achieving the minimum. Ties define switching surfaces in $\mathcal{S}^{\text{phys}}$.

