Appendix I — Proof of Corollary 1 (Dominance condition)

Corollary I.1 (restatement of Corollary 1). Under the setup of Theorem H.1, the capacity rule dominates fiat under C2, i.e. $L_P^{(C2)} < L_F^{(C2)}$, iff
\Delta V + \Delta D < 0,


where
\Delta V = [\operatorname{Var}(\Delta m^C) - \operatorname{Var}(\Delta m^F)] - 2[\operatorname{Cov}(\Delta m^C, \Delta y) - \operatorname{Cov}(\Delta m^F, \Delta y)],


\Delta D = (\mu_m^C - \mu_y - \pi^*)^2 - (\mu_m^F - \mu_y - \pi^*)^2.

Proof. By Theorem H.1 applied to each rule:
L_P^{(C2)} - L_F^{(C2)} = [\operatorname{Var}(\Delta m^C) - \operatorname{Var}(\Delta m^F)] - 2[\operatorname{Cov}(\Delta m^C, \Delta y) - \operatorname{Cov}(\Delta m^F, \Delta y)] + [(\mu_m^C - \mu_y - \pi^*)^2 - (\mu_m^F - \mu_y - \pi^*)^2].


The $\operatorname{Var}(\Delta y)$ terms cancel in the difference. $L_P^{(C2)} < L_F^{(C2)}$ iff the sum is negative. $\square$

Remark I.1 (Sign reading). The condition decomposes into three effects:

· Variance channel: $\operatorname{Var}(\Delta m^C) < \operatorname{Var}(\Delta m^F)$ is favorable.
· Covariance channel: $\operatorname{Cov}(\Delta m^C, \Delta y) > \operatorname{Cov}(\Delta m^F, \Delta y)$ is favorable (money growth that co-moves with output reduces inflation variance).
· Drift channel: $|\mu_m^C - \mu_y - \pi^*| < |\mu_m^F - \mu_y - \pi^*|$ is favorable. If the capacity index grows faster than output (e.g., exponential compute), $\mu_m^C$ drifts up and the drift channel penalizes the capacity rule.

The compute and capital dynamics of §5 enter through $\mu_m^C$, not through $\operatorname{Var}$.

