Appendix J — Note on the relation between the radial function and the Minkowski gauge

The Minkowski functional of a set $F \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is
p_F(y) = \inf\{\lambda > 0 : y \in \lambda F\}.


For $F$ convex, closed, containing the origin, and bounded, $p_F$ is a norm-like function with $F = \{y : p_F(y) \leq 1\}$.

The radial function of $F$ in direction $y^*$ is
\rho_F(y^*) = \sup\{\lambda > 0 : \lambda y^* \in F\}.

Proposition J.1. For $F$ closed, containing the origin, and star-shaped about $0$, with $y^* \neq 0$:
p_F(y^*) = \frac{1}{\rho_F(y^*)}, \quad \text{provided } \rho_F(y^*) \in (0, \infty).

Proof. $y^* \in \lambda F$ iff $y^*/\lambda \in F$ iff $\lambda \geq 1/\rho_F(y^*)$ (using star-shapedness to write $F = \{\lambda y^* : \lambda \in [0, \rho_F(y^*)]\}$ intersected with other directions). Thus $p_F(y^*) = \inf\{\lambda : \lambda \geq 1/\rho_F(y^*)\} = 1/\rho_F(y^*)$. $\square$

Remark J.1. The two objects carry the same information for star-shaped $F$. We use $\rho$ because it is the object that directly scales the reference bundle: $\rho_F(y^*)$ has the interpretation "the largest multiple of $y^*$ that is feasible," which is the natural capacity measure. The Minkowski gauge $p_F(y^*) = 1/\rho_F(y^*)$ has the interpretation "the smallest scaling of the unit ball needed to contain $y^*$," which is less natural in this context.

Remark J.2. v3.1 uses "radial function" throughout, and refers to the Minkowski gauge only in this appendix.

