Appendix K — Existence and stability of the steady-state Kalman filter

Proposition K.1. Consider the state-space model of §6.1, §F.1. Assume:

(i) $\sigma_\mu^2, \sigma_g^2, \sigma_c^2 > 0$.

(ii) $0 < \phi < 1$.

(iii) The pair $(F, H)$ is detectable and the pair $(F, Q^{1/2})$ is stabilizable.

Then the Riccati difference equation admits a unique positive-definite steady-state solution $\Sigma$, and the corresponding Kalman gain $K = \Sigma H'(H\Sigma H')^{-1}$ stabilizes the filter.

Proof. Under (i)–(iii), the standard conditions for existence and uniqueness of the steady-state solution to the algebraic Riccati equation are satisfied (Kailath, Sayed, Hassibi 2000, Ch. 5, Thm 5.4.1). Detectability of $(F, H)$: the observability matrix has rank 3 for $\phi \neq 1$ (the states are $\mu, g, c$; $\mu$ and $g$ are observed through $p$, and $c$ through the difference $p_{t+1} - \mu_{t+1} - g_{t+1}$, which is identifiable given the local-linear structure). Stabilizability of $(F, Q^{1/2})$: since $Q > 0$, all modes are directly excited. $\square$

Remark K.1 (Degenerate case). If any $\sigma_i^2 = 0$, the Riccati equation may admit only a semi-definite solution and the filter is deterministic. The steady-state $\Sigma$ is then singular and the variances $A, B$ in Theorem 4 may vanish, invalidating strict convexity. We therefore exclude the degenerate case by (i).



